 We have

\begin{align*}
\left(a+\frac{1}{b}\right)\left(\frac{1}{b}-a\right) &= \frac{1}{b^2}-a^2-1 \\
&= \frac{1}{b^2}-a^2-1.
\end{align*}

Since $-6\leq a \leq -2$, we have $a^2\geq 4$. Since $3\leq b \leq 5$, we have $\frac{1}{b^2}\leq \frac{1}{9}$. Therefore,

\begin{align*}
\frac{1}{b^2}-a^2-1 &\leq \frac{1}{9}-4-1 \\
&= -\frac{36}{9} \\
&= -4.
\end{align*}

Therefore, the greatest possible value of $\left(a+\frac{1}{b}\right)\left(\frac{1}{b}-a\right)$ is $-4$.

\boxed{-4}
